---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import homepageImg from "@/assets/projects/creative-printing/creative-printing.png"

const project = getProject("creative-printing-order-flow")!

const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
const pictureWidths = [640, 960, 1280]
const pictureSizes = "(min-width: 768px) 960px, 100vw"
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="Creative Printing & Client Web Systems"
  seoDescription="Creative Printing’s order-entry portal, 20+ responsive client websites, print-to-web brand systems, and promotional email campaigns."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Alongside print production at Creative Printing, I built and maintained 20+
    responsive client websites, print-to-web brand systems, and promotional
    email campaigns for the shop and its clients. Highlighted here is Creative
    Printing’s own homepage and order-entry portal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shop needed a homepage that helped customers start the right type of
    order right away, whether copies, business cards, envelopes, wide-format
    signs, multimedia conversions, or website services. Each service needs its
    own intake questions, so a single generic form wasn’t practical.
  </p>

  <h2>The Category Grid</h2>
  <p>
    I built the homepage in WordPress with Elementor and organized it around
    placing an order. The main service categories sit in an icon grid, labeled
    with the words customers actually use rather than shop jargon, and each tile
    goes straight to the right service page or intake form.
  </p>

  <div class="order-flow" aria-label="Creative Printing order flow">
    <div class="order-flow__node">
      <span>Homepage</span>
      <strong>Order Now</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="order-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="order-flow__node">
      <span>Category grid</span>
      <strong>Recognize the job type</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="order-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="order-flow__node">
      <span>Next step</span>
      <strong>Service page or intake form</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure class="case-figure">
    <Picture
      src={homepageImg}
      alt="Full Creative Printing homepage: logo and phone number, main navigation, an illustrated storefront hero with an Order Now button, the eight-tile order grid, and a location map."
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
    />
    <figcaption>
      The full homepage: an Order Now button in the hero, the eight-tile service
      grid, and a location map.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Seven years later, the category grid still anchors the live site, and the
    same system-first approach carries across the shop’s client web builds,
    print-to-web brand work, and promotional email campaigns.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .order-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }

  .order-flow__node {
    border: 2px outset var(--stone-light);
    padding: 0.625rem 0.75rem;
    background: var(--secondary);
    font-family: var(--font-arial);
  }

  .order-flow__node span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    color: var(--heading);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .order-flow__node strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .order-flow__connector {
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.25rem;
    margin-left: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--stone);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .order-flow {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 2rem minmax(0, 1.2fr) 2rem minmax(
          0,
          1fr
        );
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0;
    }

    .order-flow__connector {
      width: 100%;
      height: 1px;
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
